section.para:
		The feedforward tagger is a small neural network over word, suffix, and word shape
		features. Train a FeedforwardPOSModel with
		FeedforwardPOSTrainer, wrap it in a FeedforwardPOSTagger,
		and call tag. The hyperparameters are passed as a
		FeedforwardPOSTrainer.Settings record in the order shown below;
		Settings.defaults() supplies a usable starting point. Training is
		deterministic for a fixed seed on a given platform and JVM.
		FeedforwardPOSTaggerUsageTest asserts the behavior shown here.

// embeddingSize, hiddenSize, epochs, batchSize, learningRate,
// l2, dropout, wordCutoff, suffixCutoff, seed
FeedforwardPOSTrainer.Settings settings = new FeedforwardPOSTrainer.Settings(
    16, 32, 80, 32, 0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 1, 1, 17L);

FeedforwardPOSModel model = FeedforwardPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, settings);
FeedforwardPOSTagger tagger = new FeedforwardPOSTagger(model);

String[] tags = tagger.tag(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});

Pretrained word vectors are an opt-in: an overload of train takes a java.util.function.Function that is asked once per distinct lowercased training word and returns its vector, or null for a word without one. The vectors of the training words extend the input layer and are stored inside the model, so tagging needs no separate embedding source and a word without a stored vector scores that block as zeros. A further overload also takes a coverage lexicon, extra words whose vectors are stored besides the training words; the lexicon does not change training itself, it only widens tagging-time coverage to words never seen in training. Without these opt-ins the two-argument train call above behaves exactly as before. FeedforwardPretrainedVectorsTest asserts the behavior shown here.
// Return one vector per word the source knows, null for a word without one. Function<CharSequence, float[]> wordVectors = word -> lookupVector(word);
FeedforwardPOSModel vectored = FeedforwardPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, settings, wordVectors);
// Additionally store vectors for words never seen in training: FeedforwardPOSModel withLexicon = FeedforwardPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, settings, wordVectors, lexiconWords);

		How confident was the tagger? topKSequences returns the greedy
		tagging as a single scored Sequence: getProbs()
		holds the model's probability for each assigned tag, one per token and each
		in the interval (0, 1], and getScore() is the sum of the logs
		of those probabilities.
		FeedforwardPOSTaggerUsageTest asserts the behavior shown here.

Sequence[] sequences = tagger.topKSequences(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});
Sequence best = sequences[0];

// One probability per token, each in (0, 1]:
double[] probs = best.getProbs();

		Save a trained model to a file and load it for tagging:

Path modelPath = Path.of("pos-feedforward.bin");
try (OutputStream out = Files.newOutputStream(modelPath)) {
    model.serialize(out);
}

FeedforwardPOSTagger reloaded = new FeedforwardPOSTagger(
    FeedforwardPOSModel.load(modelPath));

String[] tags = reloaded.tag(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});

		The BiLSTM tagger scores each token from whole-sentence context through a
		character-level BiLSTM, a word-level BiLSTM sentence encoder, and a linear
		tag scorer. Train a BilstmPOSModel with
		BilstmPOSTrainer, wrap it in a BilstmPOSTagger,
		and call tag.
		BilstmPOSTaggerUsageTest asserts the train-then-tag workflow
		shown here.

BilstmPOSTrainer.Settings settings = new BilstmPOSTrainer.Settings(
    16, 8, 8, 16, 60, 8, 0.05, 5.0, 0.0, 1, 16, 17L, 1, 0.0, 0, false, 1,
    0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, false);

BilstmPOSModel model = BilstmPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, settings);
BilstmPOSTagger tagger = new BilstmPOSTagger(model);

String[] tags = tagger.tag(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});

The Settings record controls the optional model components. A linear-chain CRF output layer (crf) decodes whole tag sequences with Viterbi instead of per-position argmax, a second stacked encoder layer (encoderLayers) deepens the sentence encoder, pretrained word vectors passed to an overload of train can enter through a learned adapter projection (pretrainedAdapter) or be fine-tuned at a fraction of the base learning rate (pretrainedTuning), and trainMultiTask regularizes the shared encoder with auxiliary tagging heads weighted by auxLossWeight. The variant below switches on CRF decoding and the second encoder layer. BilstmPOSTaggerUsageTest asserts the behavior shown here.
// Identical to the settings above except for CRF decoding and encoderLayers=2. BilstmPOSTrainer.Settings accurate = new BilstmPOSTrainer.Settings( 16, 8, 8, 16, 60, 8, 0.05, 5.0, 0.0, 1, 16, 17L, 1, 0.0, 0, true, 2, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, false);
BilstmPOSTagger tagger = new BilstmPOSTagger( BilstmPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, accurate));
String[] tags = tagger.tag(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});

		Save the trained model and load it without retraining:

Path file = Path.of("pos-bilstm.bin");
model.serialize(file);

BilstmPOSModel loaded = BilstmPOSModel.load(file);
BilstmPOSTagger tagger = new BilstmPOSTagger(loaded);
String[] tags = tagger.tag(new String[] {"the", "cat", "barks"});

		For a CRF model, topKSequences returns one best tagging with
		posterior probabilities for the assigned tags. These are token probabilities,
		not the probability of the complete tag sequence. CRF calculations remove
		common score offsets before computing probabilities; non-finite scores cause
		IllegalStateException. BilstmCrfUsageTest checks
		tagging and probabilities after model serialization and loading.

String[] tokens = {"the", "cat", "runs"};
Sequence best = tagger.topKSequences(tokens)[0];
String[] bestTags = best.getOutcomes().toArray(String[]::new);
double[] tagProbabilities = best.getProbs();

Provide vectors with a lookup function. This example uses small arrays to show the interface and stores an additional word for use after training:
Map<String, float[]> vectors = Map.of( "cat", new float[] {0.5f, -0.25f}, "dog", new float[] {0.25f, 0.5f}, "kitten", new float[] {0.4f, -0.2f});
BilstmPOSModel model = BilstmPOSTrainer.train(sampleStream, settings, word -> vectors.get(word.toString()), List.of("kitten"));
A null lookup result means that the word has no vector. The provider must return at least one vector across the training words and additional lexicon. BilstmPOSTrainingInputTest checks this example.
